1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,528
Show the electronics, not just the box.

2
00:00:03,205 --> 00:00:05,548
This Arduino Nano Matter case is designed

3
00:00:05,548 --> 00:00:08,606
with OpenAI Codex in real FreeCAD, recorded

4
00:00:08,606 --> 00:00:09,640
with OBS.

5
00:00:10,317 --> 00:00:12,332
The official three D board model stays

6
00:00:12,332 --> 00:00:13,450
seated inside.

7
00:00:14,378 --> 00:00:16,661
First, reconcile the sources.

8
00:00:17,339 --> 00:00:19,390
The mechanical drawing and official STEP model

9
00:00:19,390 --> 00:00:21,332
agree on a forty-three point one eight

10
00:00:21,332 --> 00:00:23,919
by seventeen point seven eight millimetre board.

11
00:00:24,596 --> 00:00:26,878
We use those mechanical dimensions rather than

12
00:00:26,878 --> 00:00:28,579
the rounded overview description.

13
00:00:29,506 --> 00:00:31,412
A shorter rounded shell leaves the last

14
00:00:31,412 --> 00:00:33,111
eight millimetres of the board outside the

15
00:00:33,111 --> 00:00:35,079
case, near the antenna end.

16
00:00:35,756 --> 00:00:37,395
Walls and floor are two point four

17
00:00:37,395 --> 00:00:38,465
millimetres thick.

18
00:00:39,142 --> 00:00:41,742
Radio performance still needs real testing.

19
00:00:42,669 --> 00:00:44,659
Two front mounting supports and two rear

20
00:00:44,659 --> 00:00:46,506
cradle rests support the board.

21
00:00:47,184 --> 00:00:49,419
Separate posts take the lid screws.

22
00:00:50,096 --> 00:00:51,822
These are prototype features;

23
00:00:51,904 --> 00:00:53,943
retention and screw choice must be checked

24
00:00:53,943 --> 00:00:55,171
with actual hardware.

25
00:00:56,099 --> 00:00:58,514
The front opening provides USB C cable

26
00:00:58,514 --> 00:00:59,220
access.

27
00:00:59,897 --> 00:01:02,033
Header slots and a central control opening

28
00:01:02,033 --> 00:01:04,475
keep the connections and both buttons reachable.

29
00:01:05,152 --> 00:01:07,288
The official component model helps us inspect

30
00:01:07,288 --> 00:01:08,237
the arrangement.

31
00:01:09,164 --> 00:01:10,839
Raise the lid while the board stays

32
00:01:10,839 --> 00:01:12,067
seated in its case.

33
00:01:12,744 --> 00:01:14,237
Now you can see the connector and

34
00:01:14,237 --> 00:01:15,695
component layout clearly.

35
00:01:16,373 --> 00:01:18,169
The board reference stays in the editable

36
00:01:18,169 --> 00:01:21,069
document, and is excluded from enclosure manufacturing

37
00:01:21,069 --> 00:01:21,873
exports.

38
00:01:22,800 --> 00:01:26,101
We check valid solids, intersections, STEP readback

39
00:01:26,101 --> 00:01:27,669
and closed print meshes.

40
00:01:28,346 --> 00:01:30,424
A physical print remains the next step,

41
00:01:30,446 --> 00:01:33,298
including cable fit, board retention, heat and

42
00:01:33,298 --> 00:01:34,659
wireless performance.

43
00:01:35,586 --> 00:01:36,899
Better references.

44
00:01:37,577 --> 00:01:38,623
Better tools.

45
00:01:39,300 --> 00:01:41,123
Get into Codex right away.

46
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:46,108
Designed with OpenAI Codex, using FreeCAD, OBS

47
00:01:46,108 --> 00:01:47,518
and paid Azure Speech.

48
00:01:48,196 --> 00:01:50,370
Special thanks to the OpenAI team.

49
00:01:51,048 --> 00:01:52,967
Find us at finnandre dot no.

50
00:01:53,919 --> 00:01:56,000
Get into Codex right away.
